{:edb/db-before-t 1938 :edb/db-after-t 1939 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535251 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:26:56.880Z" 13194139535251 true] [17592186220649 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220651 13194139535251 true] [17592186220693 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220695 13194139535251 true] [17592186220697 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220698 13194139535251 true] [17592186221378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339874 13194139535251 true] [17592186221378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339880 13194139535251 true] [17592186221379 1020 #edb/ref 17592186339886 13194139535251 true] [17592186221945 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535251 true] [17592186221945 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y,\\,y(1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" 13194139535251 true] [17592186221945 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = x + y, y(1) =-1$\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(2)$, rounded to two decimal places.$1.03$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535251 false] [17592186221945 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+y+({x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(x+y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+3y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+3y+{x}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+3y+{x}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1+1=2.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+3y+{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =1+3\\cdot (-1)+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈0.00.$" 13194139535251 true] [17592186221952 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535251 true] [17592186221952 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-3y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2})$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{3}{2})≈{{field-1}},\\,y(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139535251 true] [17592186221952 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2x - 3y, y(1) = 2$\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ and $y(2)$, rounded to two decimal places.$-1.03$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" 13194139535251 false] [17592186221952 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x-3y)+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)-\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2},\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot 2+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{7} \\\\  & ≈1.14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{3}{2})≈1.00,\\,y(2)≈1.14.\n$$" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339874 1017 #edb/ref 17592186339875 13194139535251 true] [17592186339874 1089 "q-339749" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339874 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535251 true] [17592186339874 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5x+3y,\\,y(-1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(1)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339874 1113 #uuid "5f65d0be-ba21-5548-be1c-19a3b018adff" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339874 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-(-1)=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(5x+3y+(5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+3{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+3{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y+3{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{y}_{\\text{new}} & =5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=-1+2=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(5x+5{x}_{\\text{new}}+4y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(5\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈2.00.$" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339876 13194139535251 true] [17592186339875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339877 13194139535251 true] [17592186339875 1000 #edb/ref 1759