}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1}{2}-0=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2y+({x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(x+2y+{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+\\frac{1}{2}+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈1.00.$" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339882 13194139535251 true] [17592186339881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339883 13194139535251 true] [17592186339881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339884 13194139535251 true] [17592186339881 1000 #edb/ref 17592186339885 13194139535251 true] [17592186339881 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535251 true] [17592186339881 1041 "field-1" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339881 1104 #edb/ref 17592186339884 13194139535251 true] [17592186339881 1110 #uuid "ca8bea33-3a84-5b7a-9945-9171d82cb0fb" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339882 1034 "1.03" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339882 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535251 true] [17592186339882 1111 #uuid "37fa1512-feac-54c2-80b4-ab3d44036ab2" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339883 1034 "1.32" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339883 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535251 true] [17592186339883 1111 #uuid "f218bcec-74b2-5179-a4ce-604e78c65bcf" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339884 1034 "1.00" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339884 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535251 true] [17592186339884 1111 #uuid "e524c457-72a2-531a-98c7-2f593c6503c9" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339885 1034 "1.50" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339885 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535251 true] [17592186339885 1111 #uuid "8cc48b38-068e-5656-839b-d35c2e1dd67b" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339886 1017 #edb/ref 17592186339887 13194139535251 true] [17592186339886 1017 #edb/ref 17592186339891 13194139535251 true] [17592186339886 1089 "q-339871" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339886 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535251 true] [17592186339886 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=25x+5y,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{5})$ and $y\\,(\\frac{2}{5}),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈{{field-1}},\\,y\\,(\\frac{2}{5})≈{{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339886 1113 #uuid "f8988821-0fed-5205-b021-83682db1c3eb" 13194139535251 true] [17592186339886 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y\\,(\\frac{2}{5}).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=\\f